  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1058 PM CDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nDry and Breezy Sunday...As a surface high pressure system shifts to \nthe southeast, return southwesterly flow will reside over the area. \nMeanwhile, a low pressure system over the Central Plains will cause \nthe pressure gradient to tighten over the forecast area, causing \nsouthwest winds to increase. Gusts of 25 to 30 mph are expected, \nwith the potential (NBM probs up to 30%) for a few gusts near 35 \nmph. The highest wind gusts will be from the mid-morning through the \nmid-afternoon, gradually subsiding into the evening. The southwest \nflow will also allow temperatures to warm into the low 60s.\n\nRest of the Week...High pressure and mid-level ridging will \nremain in place for the first half of the work week, keeping \nconditions dry. Guidance continues to decrease with the next \nchance for precip midweek, given the lack in forcing and moisture \nfrom the nearby cold front. LREF probs and NBM probs are both \nshowing less than 10% for measurable precip (0.01 inches). Mid-\nlevel ridging and high pressure then return for the remainder of \nthe week, keeping conditions dry. This pattern will also cause \ntemperatures to remain above normal and gradually warm each day by\na couple degrees, potentially reaching the low 70s for some spots\nby Wednesday and hovering around there through the remainder of \nthe week.\n\nDue to recent rainfall, fine fuels are projected to be only \nmarginally receptive to risk of wildfires even with the warmer \ntemperatures. Will have to keep eye on this though given the \nincreasing amount of dead fine fuels with recent sub freezing\ntemps.",
